# Troubleshooting Layer Conventions

`troubleshooting/` holds question-and-answer articles. Each article answers one
question a photographer types into search. Copy mechanics are in
`help-mdx-copy.mdc`; tone of voice in `.agents/skills/voice-review/tone.md`.

## Structure

```text
troubleshooting/
├── index.mdx                 # sidebarTitle: Overview
└── <question-slug>.mdx       # kebab-case, from the question's key nouns
```

- Pages sit in top-level `groups` under the Troubleshooting tab in
  `config/navigation/index.json`. The first group is `Troubleshooting` and
  holds `troubleshooting/index` only; its repeated tab heading stays hidden
  through the existing sidebar styles. The remaining groups match the
  overview's topic headings and keep every question visible.
- `title` is the question in plain words, sentence case, question mark
  optional (`Why can't my client download photos`).
- `description` is the one-sentence answer or the cause.

## Overview

- `index.mdx` lists every question article under short topic headings that
  match what a photographer is doing, such as viewing or downloading photos.
- Use one `<Card>` per question, with the article's title, a local icon, and
  a short description of what the answer helps with. List each question once.
- Keep the sidebar's topic names, question membership, and order aligned
  with the overview. When adding a question article, add it to the matching
  sidebar group and add its card to the matching topic on the overview too.

## Patterns

```mdx
---
title: Why can't my client download photos
description: Downloads are off for the gallery share, or the download limit has been reached.
---

Start by checking the gallery share's download settings.

## Downloads are turned off for the gallery share

Turn on the download option your client needs in the gallery share.

<Card title="Allow downloads" icon="/assets/icons/sent.svg" href="/guides/gallery-shares/downloads">
  Choose what your clients can download from a gallery share.
</Card>

## The download limit has been reached

Raise the relevant limit in the gallery share's download settings.

```

- Start with a short introduction explaining what to check. Lead with the
  most common cause, then use one visible `##` heading per cause and a fix in
  one to three sentences. Keep the main checks visible without `<Accordion>`.
- A single-cause article answers directly in prose, with headings only when
  they help explain the answer.
- Point to the Guides article for the full steps with a `<Card>` instead of
  repeating them. Give the card the article's title, a local icon, and a short
  sentence explaining what the reader can do there.
- Put a card in the relevant section, after the answer. For a single-cause
  article, put cards after the answer prose. Avoid repeating the same guide
  card across nearby sections. Use the same title and icon whenever linking
  to the same article.
- Do not use inline article links or a `## Related` bullet list. Do not
  repeat cards in a footer when they already appear beside the relevant cause.
- End with how to reach support when the fix does not apply:
  Open **Help**, then click **Chat with support**. Do not publish an email address.

## Boundaries

- No feature walkthroughs. If the answer needs more than three steps, write a
  Guides article and link it.
- No internal causes (server errors, queue names, status codes). Describe
  what the photographer or client sees.
- No `<Steps>` here unless the fix is genuinely sequential and has no
  article in Guides.
